CodeDev runs Next.js 15 (App Router). To speed up /courses/[slug], a teammate proposes:

// lib/progress.ts
export async function getCachedProgress(slug: string) {
  const session = await getSession();
  return unstable_cache(
    () => getProgress(session.userId, slug),
    ["progress", slug],
    { revalidate: 60 },
  )();
}

Statement: "Because getProgress is called with session.userId, every learner gets a separate cache entry."

// lib/course.ts
import { cache } from "react";

export const getCourse = cache(async (slug: string) => {
  console.log(`[db] select course ${slug}`);   // CodeDev's own log line
  return db.course.findBySlug(slug);
});

The layout, the page and generateMetadata of /courses/[slug] each call getCourse with the same slug. The route is dynamic (it reads the session cookie). The server log for two visits to /courses/react-basics:

[req 1] GET /courses/react-basics
[db] select course react-basics
[req 2] GET /courses/react-basics
[db] select course react-basics

Statement: "This log is what React cache() is expected to produce: it removes the duplicate calls inside one request, but it does not share the result between requests."
